---
/**
 * The `Emails` section of `/handoff/` — the three transactional emails, rendered so they can be
 * looked at rather than read as source.
 *
 * ── IT IS A SECTION, NOT A SURFACE IN THE REGISTRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 * `handoff-registry.ts` models a FLOW: an ordered walk of steps, each with a URL you can open and a
 * state that URL genuinely produces. An email has neither. There is no address, no state to open,
 * and the page's own load-bearing rule — a link here always produces the state it names — would have
 * to be answered with `no URL yet` on every single step. That is precisely the noise the 2026-08-21
 * rebuild removed, and adding an eighth surface would put it back under a new name.
 *
 * The precedent is already on the page twice: `Cross-cutting` and `Platforms` are both sections that
 * are not flows, both render from their own data, and neither is a `HandoffFlow`. This is the third.
 * So `HANDOFF_FLOWS` is untouched, the `n flows` count in the header stays true, and this section
 * reads its own three items from `lib/email-previews.ts`.
 *
 * ── WHY AN IFRAME, WHEN §5 OF THE HANDOFF DESIGN DOC ARGUES AGAINST THEM ───────────────────────
 * It argues against framing PORTAL SURFACES, and it is right about those: the four `?platform=`
 * frames were removed because the element being compared sat below the fold of a 560px cell, every
 * cell showed the same site header, and the served HTML of all four addresses was identical anyway.
 * A frame there cost a screenful to show nothing.
 *
 * An email is a different kind of artefact. It is a standalone HTML document with its own `<head>`,
 * its own reset and a table layout built for clients that have no cascade worth the name. Rendered
 * inside this page it would inherit the brand bridge, the page's font stack and the theme — none of
 * which any mail client will ever load — and the reviewer would be judging a thing that does not
 * exist. Here the frame IS the fidelity, where there it was the obstruction. Same mechanism, opposite
 * verdict, because the subject changed.
 *
 * `srcdoc` rather than a URL, so the frame carries the COMPILED FILE and cannot drift from it.
 * Copying the three files into `public/` would work and would create a third copy to go stale.
 *
 * `sandbox="allow-same-origin"` — scripts inside the frame are refused (an email has none, and one
 * appearing is worth failing on), while the parent keeps the access it needs to size the frame.
 *
 * ── 600px IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A TASTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * MJML's own default body width, and what every email template is built to, because it is what
 * survives Outlook's reading pane. At this page's width the reviewer would be shown a layout nobody
 * receives.
 *
 * ── NOTHING HERE IS EVIDENCE ABOUT A MAIL CLIENT ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 * This is a browser rendering an email's HTML. Gmail rewrites it, Outlook on Windows renders it
 * through Word. The page says so where the reviewer reads it, not only in this comment.
 *
 * NO ASTRO COMMENT MAY GO INSIDE A TEMPLATE EXPRESSION BELOW — six SSR 500s with empty bodies are on
 * record from exactly that, and every gate passes them.
 */
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import { EMAIL_PREVIEWS, EMAILS_NOT_BUILT } from '../lib/email-previews';
---

<section class="em-surface" id="surface-emails" data-hf-section data-hf-name="emails email templates acknowledgement reply login link">
  <h2 class="em-h2">
    <Icon name="mail" size={18} />
    Emails
    <span class="em-h2-n">{EMAIL_PREVIEWS.length} templates</span>
  </h2>

  <p class="em-lede">
    The mail a person gets after using the form above. In an email-first product this is the half of
    the experience they actually spend time in, so it belongs on this page beside the screens.
    Each frame is the <strong>compiled file that would be sent</strong>, shown at
    <strong>600px</strong> — the width every email is built to, because it is what survives Outlook's
    reading pane.
  </p>

  <p class="em-lede">
    <strong>These are designed, not wireframes.</strong> A letter on a near-white ground: the logo
    standing on the card, the letter, and a two-line close. Colour marks three things and no fourth:
    identity, what can be acted on, and the one string the reader copies.
  </p>

  <p class="em-lede">
    <strong>Every email ships two drawings and two palettes</strong>, and the viewer has a toggle for
    each. <em>Style 1</em> is the plain-type letter; <em>Style 2</em> is the instrument, with mono
    labels, the reference in corner brackets and a stage track. The words are held still across the
    pair on purpose: change the copy and the form at once and whichever wins, nobody can say which
    change won. The frames on this page are Style 1; open the viewer for the other.
  </p>

  <p class="em-lede">
    <strong>What is still unverified is the mail client.</strong> Nobody in this repo has opened any
    of these in Gmail or in Outlook on Windows, and the browser cannot answer for either. Those two
    need a person with real accounts; the readme names the three values to look at first, and the
    masthead's blocked-image fallback is on that list.
  </p>

  <p class="em-lede">
    The frames below are filled with the portal's own case &mdash; reference BO-7QX9-K4TD, Dana
    Keller at Northwind &mdash; lifted verbatim from <code class="em-code">data/tickets.ts</code>, so
    the mailbox and <code class="em-code">/requests/</code> tell one story. The variable list under
    each email is built from the <em>raw</em> file, so it still names everything the sender has to
    supply.
  </p>

  <p class="em-lede">
    Colour marks three things and no fourth: <strong>identity</strong> (the logo),
    <strong>what can be acted on</strong> (the button, every link), and <strong>the one string the
    reader copies</strong> (a full-bleed band). Which band says how much it matters: the
    acknowledgement's reference gets the soft-primary teal because it is the payload, the login
    link's URL gets a neutral band because it is only the fallback for the button above it, and the
    reply gets no band at all because a person wrote its payload and nothing there is for copying.
  </p>

  <p class="em-lede">
    <strong>What is still unverified is the mail client.</strong> Nobody in this repo has opened any
    of these in Gmail or in Outlook on Windows, and the browser cannot answer for either. Those two
    need a person with real accounts; the readme names the three values to look at first, and the
    masthead's blocked-image fallback is on that list.
  </p>


  {/* THE VIEWER IS THE OTHER HALF. This section is the overview — three emails at once, each with
      its subject, its variables and its plain-text part. `/handoff/emails/` is for looking at ONE of
      them properly, at a chosen width, with nothing else on screen. Neither can do the other's job
      without getting worse at its own. In-page anchor rules do not apply: this leaves the page, so
      it carries `target` by hand like every other outbound link here. */}
  <p class="em-lede">
    <strong>The browser is not a mail client.</strong> Nothing on either page says how these look in
    Gmail, whose dark theme inverts colours on its own terms, or in Outlook on Windows, which renders
    through Word and reads no media query at all. Both need a person with real accounts. This
    paragraph used to sit under the viewer; it belongs here, with the rest of the writing about these
    emails, so that the viewer ends in the email rather than in three paragraphs of ours.
  </p>

  <p class="em-viewer">
    <a href="/handoff/emails/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      <Icon name="monitor" size={14} />
      <span>Open the viewer — one email at a time, desktop or mobile width</span>
      <Icon name="external-link" size={13} />
    </a>
  </p>

  <p class="em-warn">
    <Icon name="triangle-alert" size={16} />
    <span>
      <strong>This is a browser, not a mail client.</strong> Nothing below is evidence about how these
      look in Gmail or Outlook. Gmail's dark theme inverts colours on its own terms and Outlook on
      Windows renders through Word — those are the two places a responsive email actually breaks, and
      both need a human with real accounts.
    </span>
  </p>

  {EMAILS_NOT_BUILT && (
    <p class="em-warn" data-missing>
      <Icon name="circle-slash" size={16} />
      <span>
        <strong>The templates have not been compiled.</strong> The frames below are empty because
        <code class="em-code">apps/support/emails/*.html</code> is missing. Run
        <code class="em-code">pnpm --filter @baseout/support emails</code> and build again.
      </span>
    </p>
  )}

  {EMAIL_PREVIEWS.map((mail) => (
    <div
      class="em-mail"
      id={`email-${mail.slug}`}
      data-hf-row
      data-hf-name={`${mail.title} ${mail.slug}`.toLowerCase()}
    >
      <h3 class="em-h3">{mail.title}</h3>
      <p class="em-purpose">{mail.purpose}</p>

      <p class="em-subject">
        <span class="em-k">Subject</span>
        <code class="em-subject-v">{mail.subject ?? 'no subject file'}</code>
      </p>

      {/* Straight into the viewer with this email already chosen — the URL carries the state, which
          is the rule the rest of this portal holds. */}
      <p class="em-open">
        <a href={`/handoff/emails/?email=${mail.slug}&width=desktop`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <span>Open {mail.title} in the viewer</span>
          <Icon name="external-link" size={13} />
        </a>
      </p>

      <div class="em-grid">
        <div class="em-frame-wrap">
          {mail.styles.one.html ? (
            <iframe
              class="em-frame"
              title={`${mail.title} — the rendered email`}
              srcdoc={mail.styles.one.html}
              sandbox="allow-same-origin"
              data-em-frame
              loading="lazy"
            />
          ) : (
            <p class="em-empty">Not compiled — run the build script.</p>
          )}
        </div>

        <div class="em-side">
          <h4 class="em-h4">Variables</h4>
          <p class="em-note">
            Read out of the compiled file, so this list cannot go stale. The syntax is
            <code class="em-code">&#123;&#123; name &#125;&#125;</code> — a convention we chose,
            because Mailgun's templating already speaks it and Cloudflare imposes none.
          </p>
          <dl class="em-dl">
            {mail.variables.map((v) => (
              <div class="em-dl-row">
                <dt><code class="em-code">{v.name}</code></dt>
                {v.note && <dd>{v.note}</dd>}
              </div>
            ))}
          </dl>

          <details class="em-text">
            <summary>Plain-text part</summary>
            <p class="em-note">
              The only version that survives a gateway that strips HTML, and the half nobody looks at.
            </p>
            <pre class="em-pre">{mail.styles.one.text ?? 'no plain-text file'}</pre>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  ))}

  <p class="em-foot">
    Source and the argument behind every line of copy: <code class="em-code">apps/support/emails/</code>.
    Each template's header comment carries its own reasoning; the readme carries the rules all three
    obey — no email names how long anything will take, the acknowledgement's subject never echoes what
    was submitted, and the author is a real name or the desk, never an invented person.
  </p>
</section>

<script>
  /* The sizing helper is SHARED with the viewer at `/handoff/emails/` — `lib/email-frame.ts`. It
     carries the collapse-before-measuring fix and the reason for it; a second copy here is how one
     of the two would keep the broken version. */
  import { watchEmailFrame } from '../lib/email-frame';

  for (const frame of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLIFrameElement>('[data-em-frame]')) {
    watchEmailFrame(frame);
  }
</script>

<style is:global>
  /* ── EVERY CLASS HERE IS `em-`, AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS ───────────────────────────────────────
     The first version of this component reused the page's `hf-surface`, `hf-h2`, `hf-lede` and
     `hf-code` so the section would match its neighbours. It rendered COMPLETELY UNSTYLED and the
     build was green: Astro's scoped `<style>` in `pages/handoff.astro` compiles every selector to
     `.hf-h2.astro-<PAGE hash>`, and a node emitted by THIS file carries the component's hash instead.
     Valid CSS, shipped, matching zero elements — the same defect this repo has recorded twice.
     Caught by reading the built HTML, not by any gate.

     The fix is a local mirror rather than making the page's rules `:global`: turning them global
     would give the whole page's headings a second, competing definition living in a component, and
     the next person to edit one of them would have no way to know. The values below are copied from
     `pages/handoff.astro` deliberately — if that page's heading scale moves, this moves with it by
     hand. `is:global` because this file also has runtime-injected iframes and a scoped selector
     would not reach a `<style>`-less child either. */

  .em-surface {
    margin-top: 40px;
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--hf-nav-h, 56px) + 16px);
  }
  .em-h2 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    padding-bottom: 8px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .em-h2-n {
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }
  .em-h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 650;
  }
  .em-lede {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    max-width: 46rem;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }
  .em-lede code {
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  .em-viewer {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-size: 13px;
  }
  .em-viewer a,
  .em-open a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    padding: 8px 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .em-viewer a:hover,
  .em-open a:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }
  .em-open {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  .em-warn {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    padding: 12px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--bo-soft-warning-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-warning-fg);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .em-warn[data-missing] {
    background: var(--bo-soft-error-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-error-fg);
  }
  /* The glyph matches the first line box rather than being nudged towards it: these rows are 13px at
     1.6, so 1.6em IS the line box, and centring in it survives a change of type scale. */
  .em-warn .bo-i {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    height: 1.6em;
  }

  .em-mail {
    margin-top: 24px;
    padding-top: 20px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }
  .em-purpose {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 4px 0 12px;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .em-subject {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  .em-k {
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }
  .em-subject-v {
    padding: 4px 8px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 13px;
    word-break: break-word;
  }

  /* The frame track is FIXED at the email's own width and the notes take what is left. A fractional
     track would let the email be squeezed, and the width is the one fact this section exists to
     show. */
  .em-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 600px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;
    align-items: start;
  }

  /* A 600px document floating on the page with no edge reads as part of the page. A mail client
     draws a boundary; so does this. */
  .em-frame-wrap {
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #ffffff; /* ds-ok — the paper the email is printed on, not a theme surface. */
  }
  .em-frame {
    display: block;
    width: 600px;
    height: 640px;
    border: 0;
  }
  .em-empty {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 24px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .em-h4 {
    margin: 0 0 4px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .em-note {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }
  .em-dl {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .em-dl-row + .em-dl-row {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }
  .em-dl dt {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .em-dl dd {
    margin: 4px 0 0;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .em-text {
    margin-top: 20px;
  }
  .em-text summary {
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .em-pre {
    max-width: 100%;
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    padding: 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .em-foot {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* Below the two-column threshold the frame stops being a grid track and becomes a scroller: the
     email's width is the fact being shown, so it is the one thing that must not be squeezed. */
  @media (max-width: 960px) {
    .em-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .em-frame-wrap {
      overflow-x: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
